<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Japji Sahib</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ਜਪੁਜੀ ਸਾਹਿਬ, Japjī Sāhib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Japji Sahib is the composition that opens the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>, the scripture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>. Its original name is Jap, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> japa, the recitation of a mantra, &quot;to utter in a low voice, repeat internally&quot;; &quot;ji&quot; and &quot;Sahib&quot; were added out of respect.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Japji Sahib is held to be the first composition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>, and it is mostly his writing, given its form by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-angad">Guru Angad</a>. Sikhs regard it as the comprehensive essence of Sikhism, of which the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a> is an expansion. Besides reciting or chanting, jap is also understood to mean to understand, as in a line of Gurbani where it refers to understanding wisdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work opens with the Mool Mantra, followed by 38 stanzas, or paudis, in different poetic metres, and ends with a final Salok by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-angad">Guru Angad</a>. Its themes are what true worship is and what the nature of God is. The first stanza holds that bathing at holy sites, silence or material gain cannot cleanse a person whose thoughts are unclean, and that purity comes from abiding in love of the divine. The second says that suffering and happiness, rebirth through karma and release from it all come by God's command.</p><p>Later stanzas say that good karma and God's grace are the gate to mukti, liberation, praise listening to the word and faith, and describe God as formless and beyond description. Hymns 21 to 27 say that all from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, the Buddha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> speak of God, yet no words can describe Him fully; Hymn 31 calls God the primal one, the pure light, without beginning or end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The scholar Christopher Shackle describes it as designed for individual meditative recitation and as the first item of daily prayer for the devout. It is the first text of the Nitnem, is chanted at morning and evening prayers in gurdwaras, and is recited at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> initiation and at cremations. It is distinct from the Jaap Sahib of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a>, which opens the Dasam Granth. Many printed editions appeared in the second half of the 19th century in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, Sialkot and Rawalpindi, and Max Arthur Macauliffe published a tentative English translation in 1897.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Japji Sahib” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>An introductory section holds the Mul Mantar, <b>Japji Sahib</b>, So Dhar (Rehras) and Sohila, composed by Guru Nanak.</blockquote><a href="/work/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Japji Sahib</b> is the composition that opens the Guru Granth Sahib, the scripture of the Sikhs.</blockquote><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a></li></ul></section>
